column26:
{noformat}Related to user story G1

Added 2026-10-20 (Yi). During the demo, the client said an administrator may not have the domain expertise to judge work: they see every running project, task and item, release, and manage the organisation, but do not annotate, review or adjudicate.

Revised 2026-10-21, after review of PR #91: who configures what follows the client's design docs. In governed_human_judgment_infrastructure_workflows.md, the Task Owner / Manager "configures projects, tasks, policies, and export expectations", and the System Administrator "controls roles, permissions, tenant configuration, and governance rules". So both roles configure projects and tasks, and only the administrator controls the organisation. ADR 023 records the model and the alternatives set aside, including the earlier split where only the administrator managed projects.

Defects in the model on main before this ticket:
1. Admin held every capability (ROLE_CAPABILITIES[ADMIN] = frozenset(GovernedAction)), so an administrator could annotate, review, approve, settle, open disputes and adjudicate.
2. Reads depended on write capabilities. Corrections (ADR 012) and a rejected AI output (issue #38) were shown to anyone holding REVIEW, ADJUDICATE or MANAGE_PROJECT. Blind-then-reveal events (ADR 016) were shown to MANAGE_PROJECT. Dispute reads needed DISPUTE, ADJUDICATE or MANAGE_PROJECT; reconciliation reads needed ADJUDICATE; task metrics needed MANAGE_PROJECT. Changing who may act therefore changed who may see.
3. Organisation administration checked the role name "admin" in admin_service.py instead of a capability, with its own membership rule.
4. The web decided from role names, although the login response already carries each organisation's actions (SCRUM-123).
5. Unguarded routes:
- POST /init/reset and POST /init/seed had no authentication, so anyone could wipe the database;
- POST /tasks/{task_id}/upload-videos checked only task access;
- POST /organizations had no check and made any caller an administrator of a new organisation;
- a dispute participant could escalate a submission through the API.

# Capabilities, nine:
- MANAGE_ORGANIZATION: organisation settings and default policy, members, invitations, roles, creating an organisation;
- MANAGE_PROJECT: create, edit, delete and archive a project, edit its policy;
- MANAGE_TASK: tasks and their lifecycle, guidelines, datasets and uploads, AI runs and predictions, reopening a finalised item;
- OVERSEE (new, read-only): every answer, correction, review, dispute and reconciliation, the full provenance including protocol events, metrics, quality and overview counts;
- RELEASE;
- ANNOTATE;
- REVIEW (including approve and settle);
- DISPUTE;
- ADJUDICATE.
APPROVE merges into REVIEW, and MANAGE_DATASET and MANAGE_PREDICTION into MANAGE_TASK. Every role held them together, so no one's access changes by the merge.
# Roles:
- admin = MANAGE_ORGANIZATION, MANAGE_PROJECT, MANAGE_TASK, OVERSEE, RELEASE;
- task_owner = MANAGE_PROJECT, MANAGE_TASK, OVERSEE, RELEASE;
- annotator, reviewer, arbitrator and dispute_participant are unchanged.
The two differ only in MANAGE_ORGANIZATION. Neither annotates, reviews, routes disputes or adjudicates.
# Reopen (R2-3) is MANAGE_TASK, held by both. A reopen decides nothing about the answer: the item returns to the open workflow, and the next round's annotators and reviewers judge it.
# Reads are named in one place (permissions.py), each checked with one membership and one role query:
- JUDGEMENT_READ_ACTIONS (REVIEW, ADJUDICATE, OVERSEE): corrections and a rejected AI output;
- DISPUTE_READ_ACTIONS (DISPUTE, ADJUDICATE, OVERSEE): dispute lists and details;
- RECONCILIATION_READ_ACTIONS (ADJUDICATE, OVERSEE): reconciliations, the Expert Gate's included. Deciding one still needs ADJUDICATE and independence;
- OVERSEE alone: protocol events, metrics, quality and overview counts, the cross-review report, the superseded lists.
# Organisation administration checks MANAGE_ORGANIZATION, through the same governed-action check as every other action and on the caller's own token. The rule that an organisation keeps at least one administrator stays on the role. The web reads actions through one helper and never checks role names.
# Unguarded routes closed:
- the init routes are deleted;
- upload-videos checks MANAGE_TASK;
- POST /organizations needs MANAGE_ORGANIZATION in an organisation the caller belongs to;
- escalating a submission needs REVIEW as well as DISPUTE.
# Record rules are unchanged for everyone, administrators included: draft ownership, no new work on a finalised item, no self-review, adjudication and settlement independence, judges don't annotate later rounds, the required-annotators limit, no canonical AI answer without a person.
# Scope stays organisation-wide. The check takes the resource, so project-scoped assignments (client Q6, deferred) can be added later without changing the rules.
# Tests:
- each role, one test per capability it holds and one it does not;
- tests where an administrator reviewed or adjudicated now give that person the judging roles;
- casebook cases EV-131 and EV-132, and EV-044 shows both roles reopening.
# Docs: the api_surfaces.md capability table, and ADR 023, which amends ADR 008, 012, 013, 016 and 022.
# The backend suite runs in parallel (pytest-xdist, pytest -n auto) in npm run check and CI. Each test builds its own database. Measured locally: SQLite 7.5 min to 3 min, PostgreSQL 37 min to 9 min.

Relation to other work:
- SCRUM-74 (PR #79) is merged first. Its integrity checks read the merged capabilities.
- SCRUM-45 (PR #80) and SCRUM-91 (PR #89) gate project update, archive and creation on MANAGE_PROJECT, which both roles hold, so neither changes because of this ticket.
- Development databases are reset after this merges, and everyone signs in again so the web reads the new actions.{noformat}

Related to user story A3

BACKEND AND FRONTEND: the seed, its fixtures and its tests; each organisation's actions in the login context; the header. W9.

The development seed (apps/hej-api/init_data.py) can't exercise the workflows we have built:
- Acme has one annotator, so no item gets two answers and a reconciliation can't be produced.
- Five accounts are named "Test <Role>", each with exactly one role. Nothing shows that one person can hold several roles (an annotator later given the reviewer role).
- The sample annotations are written straight to the database by Alice (admin) and Bob (reviewer), who couldn't submit answers through the workflow.
- No item's source resolves, so the work panel shows "Source preview unavailable".
- Nothing on screen says what a person may do, so with several roles, or several organisations, people find out only by being refused.

Testing a dispute, a held split, a reconciliation or a sampled second review means building it by hand each time.

# The cast: 11 named people in Acme (Alice to Kevin). Alice, Bob and Charlie keep their emails and passwords; the five "Test" accounts are replaced by:
#* Dana (annotator);
#* Ethan (annotator + reviewer);
#* Fiona (reviewer);
#* George (reviewer + dispute participant);
#* Hannah (arbitrator);
#* Ivan (arbitrator + reviewer);
#* Julia (dispute participant);
#* Kevin (task owner).
#* Charlie joins Acme as an annotator too.
#* That gives 3 annotators, 5 reviewers, 2 dispute participants and 2 experts.
# A person with several roles may do what any of their roles allows, and the independence rules still apply to them. No permission change: one role assignment per role.
# The existing samples, drafts and predictions are authored by people who could have made them (Acme annotators, confirmed by reviewers; predictions by the task owner). The existing projects stay.
# Five test projects, one per review policy:
#* A "Single review" (one approval, splits held);
#* B "Dual sign-off, manual review";
#* C "Dual sign-off, automatic dispute";
#* D "Sampled second review" (one approval, 50% sampled);
#* E "Task lifecycle".
# Each project's tasks are named for their situation:
#* 1 submission per item, and 2 submissions per item;
#* a judgement task (verdict and rationale) and a text-span task;
#* tasks in draft, paused and completed;
#* an AI-assisted task left in draft for whoever has a model configured.
#* Every item's text opens in the work panel.
# In each task, some items are untouched for testing. The others are staged through the real route functions, as the person named, in the state their reference names (e.g. a1-09-open-dispute):
#* a saved draft, awaiting review;
#* returned, rejected, and a reviewer's correction;
#* an open dispute, and disputes decided accept, return and reject;
#* a held split, and one routed by hand;
#* a dispute the platform opened;
#* an open reconciliation, and a resolved one;
#* awaiting a second approval;
#* sampled and unsampled answers;
#* a finalised item to reopen.
# Project D is staged by the actual draw: the seed reads which answers were sampled and names each item after the state it ends in.
# A staged step the routes refuse fails the seed loudly, naming the item and the person. The seed runs repeatedly, with and without --reset.
# The login response lists each organisation's actions: the governed actions its roles grant there, from the same role-to-action table the API's routes check, so the web never keeps its own copy.
# The header shows what the signed-in person can do where they are:
#* inside a project or task, that project's organisation and its actions (e.g. Annotate, Review, Route disputes);
#* outside a project, their only organisation's actions, or "N organisations" opening one line per organisation, searchable from five;
#* an admin's actions read as "Every action"; actions are never added up across organisations;
#* a login from before this change shows roles until the person signs in again.
# The root README lists the cast's logins, and the API README has a "what to test where, as whom" guide, including how to read the cross-review report, which has no screen yet. The /init route lists the new logins.
# Tests on SQLite and PostgreSQL:
#* the cast and their roles, and a person with two roles may do what either allows;
#* every staged item ends in the state it is named for;
#* the independence cases on the seed (Bob can't decide a1-09 and Ivan can; Ethan can't review an item he answered; Julia routes but can't decide);
#* each organisation's actions in the login context equal what the API allows, for every seeded person;
#* the seed runs twice without error;
#* the existing seed tests still pass.
# Web tests: the header inside and outside a project, one and several organisations, the search, an admin, and a login from before.

Out of scope: new workflow behaviour; the evaluation casebook (it builds its own world and never reads the seed); an access page or admin view of actions; a screen for the cross-review report.

After merging: every teammate runs init_data.py --reset once to get the new cast (already owed after #59 and #48), then signs in again so the header shows their actions.

Collaboration:
- Everyone testing locally: the new logins replace annotator.test, reviewer2.test, owner.test, arbitrator.test and dispute.test.
- SCRUM-103 (#64, merged): its dispute pages and links have cases to show.
- SCRUM-51 (Parth, done): project D shows its sampling and blind second review.

{noformat}Related to user story I2

BACKEND (evaluation). Hanchen. Planned for the mid-semester break, carried into W9 (PR #51). Rewritten 2026-10-01; updated 2026-10-10. Builds on PR #39 (SCRUM-68).

A system demonstrated on easy items proves nothing about the ones that matter. This ticket fixes the case template and writes the brief's adversarial categories as real cases; SCRUM-71 (W10) grows the casebook to 40.

# The case template: PR #39's TOML format plus a category from a fixed list, a one-line purpose, the gold reference it uses (dataset and source record id) and the expected outcome.
# One case or more for each category the brief names:
#* a confidently wrong AI suggestion;
#* a genuinely ambiguous item;
#* reviewers who disagree with no guideline to resolve it;
#* a guideline changed after annotation;
#* an item with missing provenance;
#* an unreviewed item reaching a proposed release;
#* a release containing a superseded item.
# Gold data comes from Group A's delivery package (dataset/text_dataset/), outside the hej repository. Copy only the records the cases use into apps/hej-api/evaluation/gold/, unchanged, with their source and record id. The reference label is never sent to the API or the AI (R1-1).
# A category whose surface does not exist yet is written now with its expected outcome and skipped with that reason until its surface lands. No case asserts today's wrong behaviour as correct.
# Every bug found in the break's scenario testing becomes a case, and its bug ticket names the case. Bugs found later are SCRUM-71's.
# An outcome can state which answer is authoritative, what it says and how many answers the item carries, read through the API; an export step can state what it carries per item.
# The runnable cases pass in CI on both databases, and CI fails if an adversarial category is left with no case.

Carried into W9 (2026-10-10):
- Jingwei's review of PR #51: a skip reported as a skip, not a failure; coverage showing how many of each category's cases run; the coverage check in CI; a gold file with a blank line, a BOM or a repeated record reported rather than crashing; a reopen forgetting the item's earlier submissions; the outcome keys made real. Done in PR #51.
- After PR #48 (SCRUM-99, 100, 101) merges, PR #51 merges main and adapts:
#* the harness's adjudicate step is #48's (submission, decision = accept | return | reject); #51's planned shape goes;
#* the driver finds a dispute the platform opened itself, which no escalate step recorded;
#* EV-012 (an expert's Reject leaves the item inconclusive) and EV-013 (a split between two reviewers opens a dispute, under the dual sign-off project) are un-skipped;
#* EV-017 states what its release must carry.
PR #48 can't un-skip them, because they are not on main. Three of the seven categories then have a case that runs.

Collaboration:
- SCRUM-68 (Jingwei): the case format; Jingwei reviews PR #51.
- PR #48 (Yi): merges first; PR #51 takes its adjudicate step.
{noformat}
